      "text": "Linux",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "qGQg7u8aN8mrctESX3O-W",
      "originalText": "Linux",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "-11USIPb1NhlWn_fcu95L",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1902.9272233576967,
      "y": -622.7578459069191,
      "width": 110,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGJ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 198703628,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 1018165040,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785344449486,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why Linux?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why Linux?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "98qg6G0a3mb8HvkCwo83e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1953.9272233576967,
      "y": -578.7578459069191,
      "width": 660,
      "height": 825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1323732788,
      "version": 7,
      "versionNonce": 533877712,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785344449486,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Imagine you're running Instagram.\n\nYou have:\n\n500 servers\nMillions of requests\nThousands of Docker containers\n\nWould you install Windows on every server?\n\nAlmost every large tech company chooses Linux because it is:\n\nStable (can run for months without rebooting)\nLightweight\nOpen source\nSecure\nHighly configurable\nExcellent for automation\n\nMost cloud servers run Linux.\n\nThat includes:\n\nAWS EC2\nDocker containers\nKubernetes nodes\nNginx\nPostgreSQL\nRedis\n\nIf you know Linux, you can work almost anywhere.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Imagine you're running Instagram.\n\nYou have:\n\n500 servers\nMillions of requests\nThousands of Docker containers\n\nWould you install Windows on every server?\n\nAlmost every large tech company chooses Linux because it is:\n\nStable (can run for months without rebooting)\nLightweight\nOpen source\nSecure\nHighly configurable\nExcellent for automation\n\nMost cloud servers run Linux.\n\nThat includes:\n\nAWS EC2\nDocker containers\nKubernetes nodes\nNginx\nPostgreSQL\nRedis\n\nIf you know Linux, you can work almost anywhere.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "I3tIBFraQ7l4z5GMtkfk5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1873.9272233576967,
      "y": 276.2421540930809,
      "width": 407,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGL",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1973182988,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1007259952,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785344449486,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What Happens When You Type a Command?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What Happens When You Type a Command?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "PG88F05g14pAgEx2hPKyV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1926.2006608576967,
      "y": 322.6835603430809,
      "width": 1067,
      "height": 1100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGM",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1499037108,
      "version": 295,
      "versionNonce": 267868624,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785344449486,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ls\nYou type:\nls -> Shell (Bash/Zsh) -> Search PATH -> Find executable-> Kernel -> Filesystem -> Display output\n\nStep 1\n\n    ls\n    The shell (Bash or Zsh) reads your input.\n\n    The shell is simply a program that accepts commands.\n    Examples:\n    \n    bash\n    zsh\n    fish\n    \n    You are not talking directly to Linux—you are talking to the shell.\n\nStep 2\n\n    The shell looks for an executable called ls.\n    How does it find it?\n    It checks the directories listed in the PATH environment variable.\n\n    echo $PATH\n\n    Output -> \n    /usr/local/bin\n    /usr/bin\n    /bin\n\n    The shell searches these directories in order until it finds an executable named ls.\n\n    Run:\n        which ls  ->  /bin/ls\n        Now you know where ls lives.\n\nStep 3\n\n    The shell executes /bin/ls.\n    That program asks the Linux kernel:\n    \"Please show me the contents of this directory.\"\n    The kernel reads the filesystem and returns the directory entries.\n    ls formats the output and prints it to your terminal",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ls\nYou type:\nls -> Shell (Bash/Zsh) -> Search PATH -> Find executable-> Kernel -> Filesystem -> Display output\n\nStep 1\n\n    ls\n    The shell (Bash or Zsh) reads your input.\n\n    The shell is simply a program that accepts commands.\n    Examples:\n    \n    bash\n    zsh\n    fish\n    \n    You are not talking directly to Linux—you are talking to the shell.\n\nStep 2\n\n    The shell looks for an executable called ls.\n    How does it find it?\n    It checks the directories listed in the PATH environment variable.\n\n    echo $PATH\n\n    Output -> \n    /usr/local/bin\n    /usr/bin\n    /bin\n\n    The shell searches these directories in order until it finds an executable named ls.\n\n    Run:\n        which ls  ->  /bin/ls\n        Now you know where ls lives.\n\nStep 3\n\n    The shell executes /bin/ls.\n    That program asks the Linux kernel:\n    \"Please show me the contents of this directory.\"\n    The kernel reads the filesystem and returns the directory entries.\n    ls formats the output and prints it to your terminal",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Sos00u2M7xPVTJ9qqdPdM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1928.9272233576967,
      "y": 419.2421540930809,
      "width": 11,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
