      "updated": 1785344449486,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is a File System?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is a File System?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ExAGYYPmIiHv2cd6eX4B2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1835.1694108576967,
      "y": 4512.992154093081,
      "width": 1122,
      "height": 850,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGa",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1495894796,
      "version": 47,
      "versionNonce": 960347600,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785344449486,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A File System is the method(software) and data structure an operating system uses to store, organize, \nretrieve, and manage files and directories on a storage device (SSD, HDD, USB drive, etc.).\n\nThink of it as a database for your files.\n\nWithout a file system, your SSD is just billions of bytes with no organization\n\nWithout a File System\nSuppose your SSD looks like this:\n101010101011010101010101001010100101010101\n\nWhere is your resume?\nWhere is your Python project?\nWhere is Windows?\nImpossible to know.\n\nWith a File System\nNow your disk has structure:\n\n/\n├── home\n│     └── amit\n│            ├── resume.pdf\n│            ├── photo.jpg\n│            └── project.py\n│\n├── etc\n│\n├── var\n│\n└── usr\n\n\nNow the OS knows exactly where every file is.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A File System is the method(software) and data structure an operating system uses to store, organize, \nretrieve, and manage files and directories on a storage device (SSD, HDD, USB drive, etc.).\n\nThink of it as a database for your files.\n\nWithout a file system, your SSD is just billions of bytes with no organization\n\nWithout a File System\nSuppose your SSD looks like this:\n101010101011010101010101001010100101010101\n\nWhere is your resume?\nWhere is your Python project?\nWhere is Windows?\nImpossible to know.\n\nWith a File System\nNow your disk has structure:\n\n/\n├── home\n│     └── amit\n│            ├── resume.pdf\n│            ├── photo.jpg\n│            └── project.py\n│\n├── etc\n│\n├── var\n│\n└── usr\n\n\nNow the OS knows exactly where every file is.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "UbPnOwAsWb7AjlpC_fsCS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1761.6655046076967,
      "y": 5448.738247843081,
      "width": 1232,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1882746036,
      "version": 259,
      "versionNonce": 1356973872,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785344449486,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What Information Does a File System Store?\n -> Suppose we have resume.pdf\n\nThe file system stores much more than just its contents.\n\nExample: -> \n\nFile Name\n↓\nresume.pdf\n\nSize\n↓\n3 MB\n\nOwner\n↓\namit\n\nPermissions\n↓\nrw-r--r--\nCreated Time\nModified Time\nLocation on Disk\nFile Type\nInode Number (Linux)\n\nThis extra information is called metadata.\n\n\nResponsibilities of a File System -> \n1. Storing Files -> photo.jpg\n2. Creating Directories -> Projects/\n3. Locating Files ->  open(\"test.txt\") in python -> The file system finds where test.txt is physically stored.\n4. Permissions -> rw-r--r-- -> file system decides, who can read, write, execute\n5. Managing Free Space -> Suppose your SSD has 1 TB , How does Linux know -> 650 GB used\n350 GB free, The file system tracks that.\n6. Recovering Data -> Many modern file systems maintain journals or logs so that after a crash they can recover \n  more safely and reduce corruption.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What Information Does a File System Store?\n -> Suppose we have resume.pdf\n\nThe file system stores much more than just its contents.\n\nExample: -> \n\nFile Name\n↓\nresume.pdf\n\nSize\n↓\n3 MB\n\nOwner\n↓\namit\n\nPermissions\n↓\nrw-r--r--\nCreated Time\nModified Time\nLocation on Disk\nFile Type\nInode Number (Linux)\n\nThis extra information is called metadata.\n\n\nResponsibilities of a File System -> \n1. Storing Files -> photo.jpg\n2. Creating Directories -> Projects/\n3. Locating Files ->  open(\"test.txt\") in python -> The file system finds where test.txt is physically stored.\n4. Permissions -> rw-r--r-- -> file system decides, who can read, write, execute\n5. Managing Free Space -> Suppose your SSD has 1 TB , How does Linux know -> 650 GB used\n350 GB free, The file system tracks that.\n6. Recovering Data -> Many modern file systems maintain journals or logs so that after a crash they can recover \n  more safely and reduce corruption.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "nphp0VuA_EV_pvzlMSNBp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1662.9272233576967,
      "y": 6494.242154093081,
      "width": 396,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGc",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1308025740,
