    {
      "id": "ZzNIJtLJxctL8LUtZPgUT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1743.5444108576967,
      "y": 8810.60152909308,
      "width": 1584,
      "height": 575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHH",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2046368048,
      "version": 54,
      "versionNonce": 1218273744,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785346497087,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Interview Questions\n\nQ1. What is an inode?\n    An inode is a filesystem data structure that stores metadata about a file, such as its permissions, \n    owner, timestamps, size, and pointers to the data blocks. It does not store the filename.\n\nQ2. Does an inode store the filename?\n    No.\n    The filename is stored in the directory.\n    The inode stores metadata and the locations of the file's data blocks.\n\nQ3. Why is renaming a 100 GB file almost instantaneous?\n    Because Linux usually updates only the directory entry (the filename to inode mapping). \n    The underlying data blocks don't move.\n\nQ4. Difference between Hard Link and Soft Link?\n    | Hard Link                                                                                     | Soft Link                                |\n    | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n    | Points directly to the inode                                                                  | Points to another pathname               |\n    | Shares the same inode as the original                                                         | Has its own inode                        |\n    | Continues to work if the original filename is deleted (as long as the inode still has a link) | Breaks if the target pathname is removed |\n    | Typically cannot span different filesystems                                                   | Can span different filesystems          |\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Interview Questions\n\nQ1. What is an inode?\n    An inode is a filesystem data structure that stores metadata about a file, such as its permissions, \n    owner, timestamps, size, and pointers to the data blocks. It does not store the filename.\n\nQ2. Does an inode store the filename?\n    No.\n    The filename is stored in the directory.\n    The inode stores metadata and the locations of the file's data blocks.\n\nQ3. Why is renaming a 100 GB file almost instantaneous?\n    Because Linux usually updates only the directory entry (the filename to inode mapping). \n    The underlying data blocks don't move.\n\nQ4. Difference between Hard Link and Soft Link?\n    | Hard Link                                                                                     | Soft Link                                |\n    | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n    | Points directly to the inode                                                                  | Points to another pathname               |\n    | Shares the same inode as the original                                                         | Has its own inode                        |\n    | Continues to work if the original filename is deleted (as long as the inode still has a link) | Breaks if the target pathname is removed |\n    | Typically cannot span different filesystems                                                   | Can span different filesystems          |\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
